    reading: "Revenue growth read in isolation says little — 8% growth is strong in a flat industry and weak in one growing at 20%. The application reads growth against the sector benchmark for the selected industry profile. For early-stage businesses, the consistency and trajectory of growth matter more than the absolute level; for mature businesses, sustained above-industry growth signals market-share gain. Declining revenue is the clearest single indicator of deteriorating competitive position, regardless of sector.",
    areas: "This metric sits at the top of the revenue-generation layer of the value chain. When revenue growth falls below the sector benchmark, the contributing factors typically originate across three areas. First, the demand pipeline: examine whether there is sufficient forward pipeline coverage to support the revenue target, whether lead-generation channels are producing at historical conversion rates, and whether the sales cycle has lengthened — a lengthening cycle with stable pipeline volume produces the same effect as a shrinking pipeline with stable cycle time. Second, the existing customer base: review whether net revenue retention is above or below 100%, because declining growth frequently reflects revenue erosion from the existing base (through churn, downgrades, or reduced purchase frequency) rather than a failure to acquire new customers. Third, competitive displacement: assess whether competitors have introduced new offerings, adjusted pricing, or entered adjacent segments that are absorbing demand that previously flowed to this business. For example, a B2B services firm experiencing growth deceleration may find that a competitor has launched a productized version of the same offering at a lower price point, or that a key referral partner has begun directing business elsewhere.",
